]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/60644"} "39 from 25 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/78407"} "Dick Fosbury"] " was an average high jumper using the standard straddle when he began experimenting with a technique in which he turned his back to the bar and cleared the bar by arching his back as he went over head-first. It would be termed the Fosbury Flop. There is some controversy as to who actually developed this first, as Canadian jumper " [:a {:href "/athletes/65821"} "Debbie Brill"] " also experimented with style in late 1966, but the technique is always named for Fosbury. Fosbury was not the favorite in 1968. Only a 2.10 jumper in 1967, he had won the 1968 NCAA Championships, but finished third at the US Trials, behind " [:a {:href "/athletes/78211"} "Ed Caruthers"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/78163"} "Reynaldo Brown"] ". But at 2.20 (7-2½), only Fosbury, Caruthers and Soviet jumper " [:a {:href "/athletes/77436"} "Valentin Gavrilov"] " were clear, all on first attempts. At 2.22 (7-3¼), Fosbury made it on his first jump, while Caruthers had a miss. Fosbury also cleared 2.24 (7-4¼) (3rd attempt) to cement the gold medal, as Caruthers could not make the height. Gavrilov would win the 1969 European Championships."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "272"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/78407"} "Dick Fosbury"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "2.14 (1)"] [:td {} "2.24 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] 